  "worked_solution": "If $X$ and $Y$ are two continuous random variables with joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ then the marginal density functions for $X$ and $Y$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y,\\,{f}_{Y}(y)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 4,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{x}\\}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14272472/assets/0a3aeba14383b64a0414f3163828d972102073dc972d64105e779b317b8574b0.png)\n\nIn our case, the marginal density function of $X$ for $0\\le x\\le 4$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{X}(x) & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}\\frac{1}{16}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{x}}x{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}{[\\frac{1}{4}x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}{[x{y}^{4}]}_{y=0}^{y=\\sqrt{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot (\\sqrt{x})^{4}-x\\cdot (\\sqrt{0})^{4}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{64}[x\\cdot {x}^{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the full expression for ${f}_{X}(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{X}(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{64}, & 0\\le x\\le 4, \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
